Chapter: PhantomBuiltins

 Four names the compiler used to promise and could not keep.

 list-files, list-directories, create-directory and write-binary-file were
 bound in the type environment and named in builtin-names, and no emitter for
 any of them was ever written. So a program using one resolved, type-checked,
 and then died at the very end of the build with CDX2040 -- an unresolved
 function offset. True, and no help at all to someone who had simply asked to
 list a directory.

 Three of the four are no longer phantoms. list-files and list-directories
 became real in Foreword chapter Fat16, and so did the write path:
 fat16-write-file and fat16-write-binary-file allocate a cluster, chain it,
 write the bytes and commit a directory entry. They are gone from
 builtin-names, so a chapter that wants them cites Fat16 and gets code that
 works, and a chapter that does not gets CDX3002 on the name -- which is the
 truth, and is what errors/fat16-write-needs-cite pins.

 create-directory was the fourth, and it is now gone the same way. It kept a
 binding in the type environment long after it left builtin-names, which is
 the worst of both: the name type-checked as a promise and then failed as
 CDX3002, Undefined name -- and that message was a lie, because the name WAS
 known. TypeEnv is the fourth table check-builtin-tables.ps1 cannot reach
 at all: it is a shadowing let-chain, not a list, so nothing caught
 the disagreement.

 The binding is removed, so CDX3002 is now the truth rather than a lie: the
 compiler does not offer create-directory as a builtin at all.

 The capability itself is delivered, and by the route this test predicted:
 fat16-create-directory in Foreword chapter Fat16 claims a cluster, zeroes
 it, writes the dot and dotdot entries and commits the parent's entry. So a
 chapter that wants to make a directory cites Fat16 and gets code that keeps
 its promise, and a chapter that does not gets CDX3002 on the bare name --
 which is the truth, and is the same shape as list-files and the write path
 before it. What is gone is the promise in the type environment, not the
 ability; the four names this chapter is about have all ended the same way.

 A name the compiler promises and cannot keep is worth less than no name.

Section: Body
  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Write] Nothing = act
    mk <- create-directory "/out"
    print-line-uni "unreachable"
  end
